==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈslɪpəɹi/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-us-slippery.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# Of a surface, having low friction, often due to being covered in a non-[[viscous]] liquid, and therefore hard to grip, hard to stand on without falling, etc.
#: ''Oily substances render things '''slippery'''.''
# {{lb|en|figuratively|by extension}} [[evasive|Evasive]]; difficult to [[pin down]].
#: ''a '''slippery''' person''
#: ''a '''slippery''' promise''
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Liable to slip; not standing firm.
#* '''1602''', [[w: William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''[[s: Troilus and Cressida|Troilus and Cressida]]'', III. iii. 84:
#*: Which when they fall, as being '''slippery''' standers, / The love that leaned on them, as '''slippery''' too, / Do one pluck down another, and together / Die in the fall.
# [[unstable|Unstable]]; [[changeable]]; [[inconstant]].
#* '''1668''', Sir John Denham
#*: He looking down<br>With scorn or pity on the '''slippery''' state<br>Of kings, will tread upon the neck of fate.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[wanton|Wanton]]; [[unchaste]]; [[loose]] in [[morals]].
#* '''1610''', [[w: William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''[[s: The Winter's Tale|The Winter's Tale]]'', I. ii. 273:
#*: My wife is '''slippery'''? If thou wilt confess –

